-- title:
--   Multiplication by n on J⁰ is locally of finite presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $N$ be a term of the structure `JZeroNeronIdentityComponent p`: this bundles a scheme $G$ with a morphism $g \colon G \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, a relative group law $L$ on $g$ (a group structure, natural in the test scheme, on the sets of sections $\{\varphi : T \to G \mid \varphi \circ g = t\}$ for morphisms $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$), a bijection `pts` from $\mathrm{JZero}\,p = \operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ onto the $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points of $g$ that is additive, Galois-equivariant and compatible with the action of the Hecke algebra through endomorphisms of $g$ respecting $L$, together with commutativity of $L$, smoothness, separatedness, local finiteness of type, quasi-compactness and surjectivity of $g$, preconnectedness of all fibres of $g$, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by each $n > 0$, a finite-index condition on the subgroup generated by the integral sections inside the Galois-fixed points, and further fields. Let $n$ be a natural number. The assertion is that the endomorphism $[n] =$ `N.L.schemeNsmul n` of $G$, namely the underlying morphism of the $n$-fold $L$-sum of the identity section $\mathrm{id}_G$ with itself (the identity section of $L$ for $n = 0$), is locally of finite presentation. Of the data carried by $N$, only the morphism $g$, its local finiteness of type, and the fact that $[n]$ is a morphism over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ enter.
--
--   This supplies, for the Néron identity component of $J_0(p)$ over $\mathbf Z$, the local finite presentation hypothesis needed alongside flatness and surjectivity of $[n]$; it is used in the construction of the primary torsion core of $J_0(p)$ and hence in the assembly of the associated Kummer-type exact sequences.
